Action, freedom, and practical reason (@action-freedom-and-practical-reason: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_36FFA1JH7A8DEDJPJEKCBS1Q4F
The philosophy of action investigates intentions, reasons, choices, habits, agency, freedom, and responsibility. This material examines how explanations of action relate to deliberation, bodily activity, social constraints, and causal accounts, including disagreements about free will and the conditions of responsible action. Routes can start with a concrete decision and deepen into competing theories. Connect these questions to ethics, social explanation, and Political-Economy while distinguishing an explanatory model of behaviour from a justification of the decision.

**Parent:** [Philosophy](../Philosophy.lingua#r_00000000000000000000001001).
